WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.550
- Today in this video
I'm going to teach you

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:05.700
a communication technique
which is extremely powerful,

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.000
which I personally use
almost every single day.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.210
You can use it for oral communication,

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:12.990
for written communication,

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.390
for all different types of contexts,

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.960
such as internal team
communication, client communication.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.450
Or also for instance in job interviews.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:24.170
It is a technique that
I've seen extensively used

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:27.410
in consulting but not so
much in other industries

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:29.940
or by other people
working not in consulting.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.820
So trust that this video
once again can help

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.270
in lifting the veil

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:37.440
and hopefully making these
techniques available to everyone

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.800
else out there as well
independent from where you work.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.870
So welcome back to another coffee break

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.950
here on my channel Firm Learning.

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:46.050
My name is Heinrich.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.780
I'm a former McKinsey
consultant and on Firm Learning

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.490
I want to help you to become
successful in the first years

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:53.630
of your career.

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:56.010
So if you like this,
please like this video,

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:57.900
and also subscribe to my channel

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.230
to stay up to date on all my content.

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:02.610
And now without further
ado, let's dive into it.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:05.877
And I will use a little
exercise to illustrate my point.

00:01:05.877 --> 00:01:10.030
And this is a type of exercise
that was quite commonly used

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:14.030
in onboarding programs like
McKinsey, BCG, or Bain,

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:17.210
because these are the situations
that you will often face.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:20.230
And I will draw from an
exercise that I already used

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:23.250
to illustrate a point
in one of my past videos

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.080
on top-down communication.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:26.310
If you've not seen it yet,

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:28.950
I can also very much recommend
to you to check it out.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.760
I will link the video somewhere above here

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:34.200
so that you can click on it
after you've watched this video.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.860
So I will now read the
scenario here from my screen.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:39.920
The situation is that you
are part of a project team

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.890
that is looking into
market entry opportunities

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:44.500
for a new project line.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.880
You conducted lots of
interviews with internal

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.270
and external experts on the topic.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:51.870
You looked into data from
the finance department,

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:55.570
from the marketing team,
and you have a first result

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:59.530
that France likely would be
a great fit for that product.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:02.680
And indeed this is because you
conducted consumer research

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:05.070
and French consumers
will likely appreciate

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.370
the offering most.

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:08.420
You looked into the competition
and currently there are

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:11.740
no strong competitors
yet involved in France.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:13.830
And you also looked into distribution

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:15.780
and warehousing capabilities,

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.800
but your company at the moment
would be very well set up

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.300
to serve the French market.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:23.162
So, so far so good,

00:02:23.162 --> 00:02:24.800
same scenarios in the last exercise.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.110
But my point that I want to
drag out here is a different one

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:31.120
because now the CEO walks
in and she asks you,

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:32.440
hey, what is going on?

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.730
What's the status with the project?

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:37.410
Can you just summarize what
your preliminary results

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:38.530
on that are?

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:41.940
So how would you answer this
and this time now I do not want

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:45.040
you to think about the top
down communication concepts,

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.760
because we've covered these
already in this other video,

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.750
but I want to focus on a very
specific different point.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.010
So if you want feel free
to pause the video now

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:56.080
and think for an answer for yourself,

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:58.240
how would you reply to such a question?

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.640
But now let's into a sample answer,

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:03.570
what could you have
said in this situation?

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.981
And here, let me just read this out.

00:03:05.981 --> 00:03:08.190
Our current hypothesis is that
France will be the best fit

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:09.750
for our product line.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:11.330
We looked into consumer research

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:13.720
and the French consumers
will like you appreciate

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:15.210
our offering most.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:18.720
The competitive landscape is
also important to consider.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.630
In France it would be the
first with this offering.

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:24.360
What's also great is that we
already have solid logistics

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:27.230
and distribution capabilities in France.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.350
So again feel free to pause this video

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:30.813
and think about this answer.

00:03:32.739 --> 00:03:34.542
Is this a good answer,
is this a solid answer?

00:03:34.542 --> 00:03:36.610
So what's the case for sure is
that this answer does comply

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:38.530
with top-down communication.

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:40.840
This important principle
that we introduced

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:41.673
in the other video.

00:03:41.673 --> 00:03:43.610
Though is this really the strongest answer

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:45.403
that you could give?

00:03:45.403 --> 00:03:47.880
And my main point of criticism
would be that this answer

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.650
feels a bit random.

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:52.930
It's a more or less random list of points.

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:54.850
It lacks structure.

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:56.610
It feels like a cloth line,

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:59.330
like the result of a
brainstorming where the person

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:02.460
was just thinking about
reasons why the French market

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.650
would be the best to end
up for the product line.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:07.560
And maybe these are indeed
all the relevant reasons

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.630
why the French market would be the best,

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:12.010
but maybe the person
also forgot to mention

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:14.310
one or two other important reasons.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:16.001
Who knows, right?

00:04:16.001 --> 00:04:17.740
So this is at least not an
answer that would give the

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:21.240
recipient a lot of confidence
that this person had

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:23.710
it really all laid out,
really clearly organized,

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:25.900
and really knew exactly
what the most relevant

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:26.800
reasons would be.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:29.270
So let's contrast that
with the second example

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:31.860
of what you could have had answered.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:34.270
So our current hypothesis is that France

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:36.450
will be the best fit for our product line.

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:38.280
This is based on three reasons.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:41.170
First consumer research
shows that French consumers

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:43.000
will appreciate our offering.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.210
Second, the competitive
landscape in France

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:46.750
is not yet developed.

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:48.770
We will be the first with this offering.

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:51.759
And third our logistics and
distribution capabilities

00:04:51.759 --> 00:04:55.130
are already set up to
serve the French market.

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:57.300
What do you think about
this answer option?

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.620
Is this a better way of answering this?

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:01.470
I would argue, yes this is the case

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:03.450
because this is answer not only follows

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:06.690
top down communication but
this answer also follows

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:10.120
the so-called rule of three.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:12.970
You are clearly starting
your answer by saying

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:15.318
that there are three reasons
for that, three points,

00:05:15.318 --> 00:05:18.170
three main arguments, and
then you are mentioning them.

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:20.600
First, second, third.

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:23.860
And I hope you agree that this
feels much more structured,

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:27.250
much more well organized than
just to randomly starting

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:28.870
to talk about a first point

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:32.150
and then later maybe a second
point and then another one

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:33.320
and another one.

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:35.200
And it all feels like brainstorming.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:37.370
When you compare the
answers next to each other,

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:38.900
they seem very similar.

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:41.560
What has been said is
very very similar indeed.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:43.960
But trust me that the
choice in example number two

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:46.810
would be perceived as
much more structured,

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:48.650
much more well organized.

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:51.420
And this is true up to the
point that in consulting

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:54.630
this was so frequently
used that it was considered

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:56.460
almost a meme.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.720
Whenever a client or partner
would be asking you a question,

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:03.760
you would likely always say
something like, yes, for sure.

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:05.530
So let me mention three points on that,

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:07.423
or yes, for sure, let me
mention three arguments

00:06:07.423 --> 00:06:09.170
where this is the case.

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:12.990
Or this is my recommendation,
and this is for three reasons.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:15.364
You will almost always try
to find a way to structure

00:06:15.364 --> 00:06:18.000
your answers in the way
that you could start them

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.790
with introducing three distinct points.

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:24.653
And yet the truth is in most
of the cases you did not really

00:06:24.653 --> 00:06:27.500
have three points like
that ready in your head

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:30.290
when you just started
beginning giving this answer.

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:33.600
You would in most cases just
start with the first reason

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:36.018
and the likeliest second reason
is what you would be able to

00:06:36.018 --> 00:06:37.810
easily come up with as well.

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:40.560
And then just on the way you
would try to come up with

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:42.720
a third poin, a third reason.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.120
And my experience is if you practice this

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:46.630
and do this regularly,

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:49.440
you will almost always be able to mention

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.310
a point number three,

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:54.300
even though maybe this third
point is not the most relevant,

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:56.510
the most important one
that you could have said.

00:06:56.510 --> 00:06:59.420
But I do know that the biggest
fear that people have when

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:02.100
answering questions like this
is that they cannot come up

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:04.750
with a third point on the way.

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:07.139
So let me share two things
that you can almost always say

00:07:07.139 --> 00:07:10.320
are the third point if we
really cannot come up with

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.362
anything else that is more
content related on the way.

00:07:13.362 --> 00:07:16.690
And the first suggestion is
to talk about next steps.

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:19.397
So what you can almost
always say as a third point

00:07:19.397 --> 00:07:21.570
is what will you do next?

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:23.370
What are the next things you will look at?

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:25.540
When is the next meeting
where you need to have

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:26.530
this all ready?

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:28.360
Talking about next steps is so easy

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:30.840
because there's almost always a next step.

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:33.100
You're almost never completely finished

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:34.950
with a piece of work on an analysis.

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:36.870
So definitely something
that should be able to

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:37.703
help you out here.

00:07:37.703 --> 00:07:40.380
And my second suggestion
will be to talk about risks

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:43.020
or downsides or doubts
that you still have.

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:45.350
So maybe you looked into
something you are coming

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:47.830
to a conclusion and you're
talking about point one

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:49.590
and point two of that conclusion,

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:52.490
but then in most cases there
will be something where maybe

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:54.460
you will need to look into further.

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:56.100
You're not a hundred percent sure about

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:59.081
or see a certain risk and
dependency where your progress is

00:07:59.081 --> 00:08:01.880
dependent on the work of another person,

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:04.000
maybe another colleague or another client.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:07.360
So these are things that you
should always be able to say

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:10.970
as a point number three if
we really cannot come up with

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:12.520
anything else that would make sense.

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:15.510
This rule of three is
really extremely powerful

00:08:15.510 --> 00:08:18.400
and very very often used in consulting.

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:21.690
By the way not only by
associates or project leads,

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:23.580
so maybe the more junior consultants,

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:26.220
but especially also by the partners.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:29.496
You will see partners and
senior partners use this rule of

00:08:29.496 --> 00:08:33.530
three all the time, more
in team communication,

00:08:33.530 --> 00:08:35.840
but also in communication to the clients.

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:38.060
So trust that this alone
should be something

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:40.230
that should make you think whether this is

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:42.380
maybe worth at least trying for you

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:44.100
for your work routines this way.

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:45.760
But of course I would also be interested

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:46.970
in your experiences.

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:49.110
What do you think about the rule of three?

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:51.030
Did you already use it in the past?

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:53.750
Where does it work or maybe
doesn't it work that way?

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:55.360
Let us know in the comments.

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:57.890
I trust this will be helpful
for every single person

00:08:57.890 --> 00:08:59.380
in the Firm Learning community.

